\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I want you to take me through the history of weak lensing research. What were the most important and fundamental papers and what was the context they were written in? Let's go one by one and discuss!\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Weak lensing has an unusual history. The core idea is almost a century old, but the field didn't really exist until the 1990s, because detecting it required measuring tiny statistical distortions in the shapes of millions of faint galaxies. Here's a roadmap of the stops I'd suggest. We can adjust it as we go.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The prehistory (1911\'961937):
\f0\b0  Einstein's light deflection, Einstein's 1936 lensing paper, and Zwicky's 1937 papers on galaxies as lenses\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The theoretical seed (1966\'961967):
\f0\b0  Kristian & Sachs, and Gunn, on how inhomogeneities distort images of distant galaxies\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The first detection (1990):
\f0\b0  Tyson, Valdes & Wenk find coherent distortions around galaxy clusters\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The theory becomes a tool (1991\'961995):
\f0\b0  Kaiser's cosmology papers, Kaiser & Squires mass mapping, and the KSB shape-measurement method\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Galaxy\'96galaxy lensing (1984\'961996):
\f0\b0  Tyson et al.'s early attempt and the Brainerd, Blandford & Smail detection\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cosmic shear predictions (1991\'961999):
\f0\b0  Blandford et al., Miralda-Escud\'e9, Kaiser, Jain & Seljak, Bernardeau et al., and Hu's tomography\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 2000 cosmic shear detections:
\f0\b0  four groups announce results almost simultaneously\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Systematics come of age (2001\'962010):
\f0\b0  intrinsic alignments (Hirata & Seljak), PSF challenges (STEP, GREAT08)\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	9	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Dark matter made visible:
\f0\b0  the Bullet Cluster (Clowe et al. 2006) and the COSMOS 3D map\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	10	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The survey era:
\f0\b0  CFHTLenS, KiDS, DES, HSC, and the S8 tension\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Stop 1: The prehistory
\f0\b0 \
The idea that gravity bends light predates general relativity. Johann von Soldner calculated a Newtonian deflection in 1801. Einstein got the same "half" value in 1911 from the equivalence principle alone. In 1915, full general relativity doubled it to 1.75 arcseconds at the Sun's limb. The 1919 eclipse expeditions led by Eddington confirmed the larger value and made Einstein famous. Soon after, people realized a massive body could act as a lens: Eddington mentioned multiple images, and Orest Chwolson described what we now call an Einstein ring in 1924.\
Einstein's own 1936 paper in 
\f2\i Science
\f0\i0 , "Lens-like Action of a Star by the Deviation of Light in the Gravitational Field," has a charming backstory. A Czech amateur named Rudi Mandl kept pestering him about stellar lensing. Einstein wrote up the calculation of the ring and the magnification, but he told the editor privately that the paper had little value. His reasoning was that for stars, the ring would be far too small to resolve, and alignments would be absurdly rare.\
Fritz Zwicky responded in 1937 with two short 
\f2\i Physical Review
\f0\i0  papers, and this is where the story really begins for us. His key move was to switch from stars to "extragalactic nebulae," meaning galaxies. Galaxies are enormously more massive, so their lensing angles would be observable and the chance of alignment far higher. Zwicky listed three payoffs:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 lensing would further test general relativity,\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 magnification would let us see galaxies too distant to observe otherwise,\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 lensing would let us 
\f2\i weigh
\f0\i0  the lensing galaxies directly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 That third point is the conceptual heart of all weak lensing. Deflection depends only on mass, not on whether the mass shines. Zwicky had argued in 1933 that the Coma cluster contained far more mass than its visible galaxies suggested, so he was primed to want a mass measurement independent of light.\
What Zwicky imagined was strong lensing: multiple images and rings. The idea that you could measure mass from subtle, statistical shape distortions of many background galaxies wasn't there yet. It also took until 1979 (Walsh, Carswell & Weymann's double quasar) for any lens to be seen at all.\
Before we move to Kristian & Sachs and Gunn, which part of this would you like to dig into: the physics of the deflection, Zwicky's reasoning, or why the field lay dormant for three decades?\
Why was the field dormant?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 It was really several bottlenecks at once, some practical and some intellectual.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The observations were out of reach.
\f0\b0  Zwicky's predicted deflections for galaxies were around an arcsecond or so, which is about the blurring caused by Earth's atmosphere. Astronomers in the 1930s\'9650s worked with photographic plates, which were insensitive and hard to measure precisely. On top of that, a strong lens needs a bright, distant source almost perfectly behind a massive foreground object. Nobody knew of a population of very distant, compact, bright sources that would make such alignments common enough to find. Without a target, there was no search.\

\f1\b The universe's scale was badly wrong.
\f0\b0  Until Baade's revision in 1952 and Sandage's work later in the decade, the Hubble constant was thought to be around 500 km/s/Mpc, several times too high. Distances were therefore underestimated and the universe seemed younger and smaller than it is. Lensing strength depends on the geometry of observer, lens and source, so any quantitative prediction rested on shaky ground. Known galaxies also sat at fairly modest redshifts, so there weren't obvious "background" sources far behind "foreground" ones.\

\f1\b General relativity was out of fashion.
\f0\b0  The physicist Clifford Will calls roughly 1925 to 1955 the "low water mark" of GR. After the excitement of 1919, it came to be seen as mathematically elegant but disconnected from experiment, with only a handful of tests and few practitioners. The rest of physics was busy with quantum mechanics, nuclear physics, and, during the war, military research. A subfield built on a neglected theory had little pull.\

\f1\b Nobody was looking for dark matter.
\f0\b0  Zwicky's lensing proposal was partly motivated by his claim that clusters contained lots of invisible mass. That claim was largely ignored for decades. It only became mainstream in the 1970s, through galaxy rotation curves (Rubin, Ford and others) and arguments about galaxy stability (Ostriker and Peebles). Without a widely felt "missing mass" problem, a method for weighing invisible matter didn't seem urgent.\

\f1\b What broke the dormancy.
\f0\b0  The 1960s changed nearly all of this. Quasars were identified in 1963, suddenly providing bright, compact sources at large distances, exactly what lensing needed. The same years brought pulsars, the cosmic microwave background, and the first Texas Symposium on Relativistic Astrophysics, which made GR a working tool for astronomers again. Theorists immediately returned to lensing: Klimov (1963) on galaxy lenses, Liebes (1964) on stellar lensing, and especially Sjur Refsdal (1964), who showed that time delays between lensed images could measure the Hubble constant and lens masses. Kristian and Sachs (1966) then asked the question that points toward weak lensing: what happens to the 
\f2\i shapes
\f0\i0  of distant galaxies when light passes through a lumpy universe?\
Even then, observation lagged. The first strong lens wasn't found until 1979, and weak lensing had to wait for two further things: CCD detectors, which from the late 1970s gave precise, linear measurements of faint objects, and the discovery (by Tyson and others in the 1980s) of a dense population of faint blue background galaxies. Those galaxies became the "wallpaper" whose tiny distortions could be averaged statistically.\
So the dormancy wasn't a lack of ideas so much as a lack of sources, detectors, a trusted cosmological scale, and a motivating problem. Shall we move on to Kristian & Sachs and Gunn, where the idea of measuring coherent shape distortions first appears?\
Yes, go ahead\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Stop 2: The theoretical seed (1966\'961967)
\f0\b0 \
These papers are the first to treat gravitational lensing not as a rare event where one object lines up behind another, but as something that happens to 
\f2\i every
\f0\i0  light ray crossing a lumpy universe. That shift is the conceptual birth of weak lensing, even though nobody used the term for another twenty years.\

\f1\b The context
\f0\b0 \
In 1965 the cosmic microwave background was discovered, and the steady state versus Big Bang debate was ending. Cosmology was becoming an observational science, but it was built on the Friedmann\'96Robertson\'96Walker models, which assume the universe is perfectly smooth and isotropic. A natural question followed: how would we 
\f2\i know
\f0\i0  from observations whether that assumption holds, and what does real lumpiness do to what we see?\
The tools for answering it came from general relativity. Rainer Sachs had developed, around 1961, what are now called the optical scalar equations. They describe how a thin bundle of light rays changes as it travels through curved spacetime, and they separate two effects:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ricci curvature
\f0\b0 , produced by matter 
\f2\i inside
\f0\i0  the beam, focuses the bundle isotropically. This magnifies an image without changing its shape.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Weyl curvature
\f0\b0 , the tidal part of gravity produced by matter 
\f2\i outside
\f0\i0  the beam, shears the bundle. This stretches a circular image into an ellipse.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 In modern weak lensing language, these are exactly 
\f1\b convergence
\f0\b0  and 
\f1\b shear
\f0\b0 . The mathematics of the field was essentially in place before anyone thought of it as a field.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Kristian & Sachs (1966), "Observations in Cosmology"
\f0\b0 \
Jerome Kristian was an observational astronomer and Sachs a relativist, and their paper in the 
\f2\i Astrophysical Journal
\f0\i0  is an attempt to do cosmology without assuming the FRW models. They expanded observable quantities (redshifts, distances, number counts, image shapes) as power series around the observer in a general spacetime. That let them ask which observations would reveal departures from homogeneity and isotropy.\
One of their results is that Weyl curvature along the line of sight would distort the images of distant galaxies, and that this distortion would be 
\f2\i coherent
\f0\i0 : neighboring galaxies would be stretched in similar directions. In principle, measuring that pattern would map the tidal field of the intervening universe. That is precisely what cosmic shear surveys measure today.\
They also saw the central difficulty. Galaxies are not intrinsically round, so any lensing distortion is buried in the random scatter of intrinsic shapes. Their estimates suggested the effect would be small, and with photographic plates it looked impractical to detect. Kristian did attempt a search for distortions in images of distant galaxies soon after, but the data of the time could not reach the needed precision. The idea had to wait for detectors and galaxy samples that took another two decades to arrive.\

\f1\b Gunn (1967)
\f0\b0 \
James Gunn, then a young theorist at Caltech (he had just coauthored the Gunn\'96Peterson paper on intergalactic hydrogen), wrote two papers on light propagation in an inhomogeneous universe. His emphasis was somewhat different. He asked how the matter along a line of sight, especially galaxies and clusters near the path, would randomly magnify or demagnify distant sources and distort their shapes.\
Gunn framed this partly as a 
\f1\b fundamental limitation
\f0\b0 : lensing by intervening matter adds noise to measurements of angular sizes and brightnesses, which cosmologists were using to measure the geometry of the universe. But he also recognized the flip side, that the same effect carries information about the mass distribution, including dark mass, between us and the source. This dual view, lensing as both nuisance and signal, still runs through the field. Supernova cosmologists today worry about lensing magnification as a noise term, while weak lensing surveys treat it as the signal.\
Gunn's work also connects to a parallel line of research. Zel'dovich (1964) had asked what happens to light traveling through a universe where matter is clumped into galaxies, so a typical beam passes through mostly empty space. Dyer and Roeder developed this "empty beam" distance in the early 1970s, and Weinberg (1976) showed that, averaged over all sources, flux is conserved, so lensing magnification and demagnification balance out. These debates about how to average over inhomogeneities foreshadowed the statistical thinking that weak lensing depends on.\

\f1\b Why these papers matter
\f0\b0 \
Kristian & Sachs and Gunn established three ideas that define weak lensing. The large-scale matter distribution distorts every distant image, not just rare aligned ones. The distortion is coherent and therefore statistically measurable across many galaxies. And the random intrinsic shapes of galaxies are the main obstacle, which means you need very large numbers of them. The first two ideas were theory. The third told everyone why it would take until 1990 to see anything.\
Would you like to look more closely at the convergence and shear distinction, since it underpins everything that follows, or move on to Tyson, Valdes & Wenk's 1990 detection?\
Move on to Tyson 1990\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Stop 3: The first detection (Tyson, Valdes & Wenk 1990)
\f0\b0 \
The paper is "Detection of Systematic Gravitational Lens Galaxy Image Alignments: Mapping Dark Matter in Galaxy Clusters," published in 
\f2\i Astrophysical Journal Letters
\f0\i0  in 1990. It's short, but it turned a twenty-year-old theoretical idea into an observational technique.\

\f1\b Three developments that made it possible
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 CCDs and automated measurement.
\f0\i0  Tony Tyson, at Bell Labs, was among the first astronomers to use CCDs for deep imaging. Just as important, he and John Jarvis built FOCAS, one of the first software systems for automatically detecting and measuring faint objects. Weak lensing needs the shapes of thousands of galaxies measured consistently, which is impossible by eye on photographic plates.\

\f2\i The "blue fog."
\f0\i0  Deep CCD counts by Tyson and Jarvis (1979) and later Tyson (1988) revealed a huge population of faint, blue galaxies, hundreds of thousands per square degree at the faintest magnitudes. Their redshifts were unknown, but their faintness and number suggested most were far away. That made them an ideal backdrop: a dense wallpaper of distant sources behind any foreground cluster.\

\f2\i Earlier failed attempts.
\f0\i0  Tyson's group had already tried. Valdes, Jarvis & Tyson (1983) searched for coherent alignments of faint galaxies across random fields, which is cosmic shear in modern terms, and found only upper limits. Tyson et al. (1984) tried to detect lensing by individual foreground galaxies and also came up empty. These null results were useful, though. They forced the group to understand the systematics of shape measurement, and they suggested that a stronger lens was needed. Galaxy clusters were the obvious choice.\

\f2\i Giant arcs.
\f0\i0  In 1986\'9687, Lynds & Petrosian and Soucail and collaborators independently found long, thin luminous arcs in galaxy clusters such as Abell 370. Paczy\uc0\u324 ski and others proposed that these were strongly lensed background galaxies, and a redshift measurement by Soucail's team in 1988 confirmed that the arc in Abell 370 lay far behind the cluster. Soon, smaller "arclets" were found too. If clusters could stretch some galaxies into dramatic arcs near their cores, then at larger radii they should stretch 
\f2\i all
\f0\i0  background galaxies slightly. Weak lensing was the natural extension of the arcs, just fainter and statistical.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 What they did
\f0\b0 \
Tyson, Valdes and Wenk imaged two clusters: Abell 1689, a massive nearby cluster at redshift about 0.18, and CL 1409+52, a more distant cluster around the radio galaxy 3C 295. They measured the orientations of the faint blue galaxies around each cluster.\
The signature they looked for is 
\f1\b tangential alignment
\f0\b0 . A mass concentration stretches background images perpendicular to the line pointing toward it, so around a cluster the galaxies should tend to line up like the rings of a target. Any single galaxy's orientation is dominated by its intrinsic shape, but averaged over many galaxies, a statistically significant tangential pattern emerged in both clusters.\
From the strength and radial behavior of the pattern, they made rough estimates of the cluster mass distributions. Their main conclusions were that the clusters contained large amounts of dark matter, with high mass-to-light ratios, and that the dark matter was distributed more smoothly than the visible galaxies. For the first time, this measured a cluster's mass through gravity's effect on light alone, independent of assumptions about galaxy velocities or hot gas being in equilibrium.\

\f1\b Its limitations
\f0\b0 \
The analysis was pioneering but crude, and the gaps set the agenda for the next few years.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No mass reconstruction method.
\f0\b0  They fit simple models to the alignment signal. There was no general way to turn a measured shear field into a two-dimensional mass map. Kaiser & Squires solved this in 1993.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Shape measurement was approximate.
\f0\b0  The telescope's point spread function smears and can itself elongate images, faking or diluting a signal. Correcting for this rigorously led to the KSB method of 1995.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Unknown source redshifts.
\f0\b0  Lensing strength depends on how far behind the lens the sources lie. With no redshifts for the faint blue galaxies, the mass normalization was uncertain, especially for the more distant cluster.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mass-sheet degeneracy.
\f0\b0  Shear alone can't distinguish a mass distribution from the same distribution plus a uniform sheet of mass, a subtlety that later work had to handle carefully.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Why it matters
\f0\b0 \
The paper showed that the Kristian\'96Sachs idea actually works: coherent shape distortions of faint galaxies can be detected and used to map invisible mass. It also established the practical recipe used ever since, which is deep imaging, a dense population of background galaxies, careful shape measurement, and statistical averaging. The theoretical community reacted almost immediately, and within a few years Nick Kaiser and collaborators had turned the method into a quantitative tool.\
That brings us to Stop 4, Kaiser's work. Would you like to go there next, or first look more closely at why tangential alignment is the signature of a mass concentration?\
What were this blue fog of galaxies? Just higher redshift galaxies?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Mostly yes, but at the time nobody knew that, and working out what they were became one of the big puzzles of 1980s\'9690s extragalactic astronomy, known as the 
\f1\b "faint blue galaxy problem."
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The puzzle
\f0\b0 \
When deep CCD counts arrived, there were far too many faint galaxies. Astronomers compared the counts to "no-evolution" models, which take the local galaxy population, place it throughout an expanding universe, and predict how many should appear at each magnitude. At faint blue magnitudes, the observed counts exceeded these predictions by factors of several. The excess galaxies were also bluer than typical nearby galaxies. Blue light means young, massive stars, so these objects were forming stars vigorously.\
Something had to give. The options were that galaxies were brighter or more numerous in the past, that there was a population of faint galaxies missing from local surveys, or that the cosmological model behind the predictions was wrong.\

\f1\b The confusing redshift surveys
\f0\b0 \
The obvious test was to measure redshifts, and the first results were surprising. Spectroscopic surveys reaching blue magnitudes of about 22 to 24, notably Broadhurst, Ellis & Shanks (1988) and Colless et al. (1990), found that the faint blue galaxies were 
\f2\i not
\f0\i0  especially distant. Their redshift distribution looked roughly like the no-evolution prediction, with few objects beyond redshift 0.5 or so, even though there were too many of them. That pointed to an excess of fairly low-luminosity, star-forming galaxies at modest redshifts, perhaps dwarf galaxies in a burst of star formation that have since faded or merged.\
This mattered for Tyson's work. If a large share of the fog sat at low redshift, then many "background" galaxies might not be far behind a cluster at all, weakening the lensing signal and making mass estimates uncertain. Tyson's interpretation relied on the fainter galaxies, beyond the reach of those spectrographs, being substantially more distant.\

\f1\b The resolution (1990s)
\f0\b0 \
Several lines of evidence settled the question over the following decade.\
Deeper spectroscopic surveys with larger telescopes, including the Canada\'96France Redshift Survey (Lilly and collaborators) and Keck surveys led by Cowie, showed that the redshift distribution climbs as you go fainter, with median redshifts approaching 1 at the depths lensing studies use. The Hubble Space Telescope, especially the Hubble Deep Field in 1995, showed that many faint blue galaxies were small, irregular and often disturbed, unlike the orderly spirals and ellipticals nearby. Steidel and collaborators' Lyman-break technique (mid-1990s) found large numbers of star-forming galaxies at redshifts around 3, whose ultraviolet light is shifted into optical bands. And Lilly et al. (1996) and Madau et al. (1996) showed that the cosmic star formation rate was many times higher in the past, peaking around redshifts 1 to 3.\
So the picture that emerged is a mixture. The fog is dominated by star-forming galaxies over a broad range of redshifts, from a few tenths out to 3 and beyond, with the typical object in a deep image at redshift around 1. They look blue because the universe was forming stars much faster then, and because redshifting moves their strong ultraviolet emission into blue filters. The "excess" reflects genuine evolution: galaxies were more actively star-forming, and probably more numerous as separate small objects before merging.\

\f1\b The legacy for weak lensing
\f0\b0 \
The episode taught the field a lesson it has never stopped relearning: the lensing signal depends on the redshift distribution of the sources, and getting that distribution wrong biases the mass. Early cluster studies had to guess it. Later work used deep spectroscopic samples and then photometric redshifts, estimated from colors in many filters. In today's surveys, calibrating source redshift distributions is one of the dominant sources of systematic uncertainty, directly descended from the question you just asked.\
Ready for Stop 4, where Kaiser and collaborators turn Tyson's detection into a quantitative mass-mapping method?\
But why were the counts so much more than no-evolution models?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The short answer is that galaxies in the past were brighter in blue light, probably more numerous as small separate objects, and the models were built on a local census and a cosmology that both undercounted what we should see. It took about a decade to sort out how much each factor contributed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 How the prediction works
\f0\b0 \
In a static, uniform, Euclidean universe, the number of galaxies brighter than a given magnitude rises steeply as you go fainter, because you are sampling a volume that grows as distance cubed. In an expanding universe, several effects flatten that rise. Distant galaxies are dimmer than their distance alone implies, the volume grows more slowly at high redshift, and redshift shifts light out of the observed band. A no-evolution model combines three ingredients: the local luminosity function (how many galaxies of each luminosity exist nearby), the spectra of local galaxy types (which set the "k-correction" for how redshift changes their observed brightness), and a cosmological model for the distance\'96volume relation. Any error in these, or any real change in galaxies over time, breaks the prediction.\

\f1\b The main explanations
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Galaxies were brighter in the past.
\f0\i0  This turned out to be the biggest factor. Star formation was much more vigorous at redshift around 1 than today. Young, massive stars are very luminous and very blue, so a galaxy forming stars rapidly outshines its present-day self, especially in blue and ultraviolet light. Brighter galaxies are seen to greater distances, which fills the faint counts with objects the no-evolution model says should be too dim. The Canada\'96France Redshift Survey (Lilly et al. 1995) showed this directly: the luminosity function of blue galaxies brightened substantially by redshift of about 0.5 to 1, while red galaxies changed much less.\

\f2\i The k-correction amplified the effect.
\f0\i0  A blue filter observing a galaxy at redshift 1 is seeing its rest-frame ultraviolet light. Old-fashioned models assumed spectra like local galaxies, which are relatively faint in the ultraviolet. Star-forming galaxies are ultraviolet-bright, so they stay visible in blue images far longer than the models expected.\

\f2\i An important clue came from infrared counts.
\f0\i0  Counts in the near-infrared K band, which traces the older stars that carry most of a galaxy's mass, were fairly close to no-evolution predictions. So the excess wasn't mainly more 
\f2\i mass
\f0\i0  in galaxies. It was more 
\f2\i star formation
\f0\i0 , which shows up in blue light. That pointed strongly to luminosity evolution rather than something exotic.\

\f2\i Small galaxies may have been more numerous.
\f0\i0  In the hierarchical picture of structure formation, today's big galaxies were assembled by merging smaller ones, so there were more separate galaxies in the past. Some theorists went further. Babul and Rees (1992) proposed a population of dwarf galaxies that had a burst of star formation around redshift 1 and then faded dramatically, making them nearly invisible today. This "disappearing dwarfs" idea was meant to explain why the Broadhurst\'96Ellis\'96Shanks redshift distribution looked unremarkable despite the excess counts. HST later showed that many faint blue galaxies were indeed small, irregular objects, although the extreme fading scenario was never clearly confirmed.\

\f2\i The local census was incomplete.
\f0\i0  The models were normalized to local galaxy surveys that may have undercounted galaxies, in two ways. Low surface brightness dwarfs are easy to miss nearby, so the faint end of the luminosity function was likely steeper than assumed. And the overall local normalization was debated; the APM survey (Maddox and collaborators, around 1990) found bright counts rising faster than expected, suggesting either a local underdensity or evolution even at low redshift.\

\f2\i The cosmology was wrong.
\f0\i0  Most models assumed a flat universe with only matter, which has relatively little volume at high redshift. A low-density universe, or one with a cosmological constant, contains substantially more volume at redshifts around 1 to 3, and therefore more galaxies. Fukugita and collaborators (1990) argued that faint counts favored a cosmological constant, years before the 1998 supernova results made it mainstream. In hindsight, a cosmological constant does account for part of the excess, though not all of it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The resolution
\f0\b0 \
By the late 1990s, the consensus was that no single cause explained it. Strong luminosity evolution in star-forming galaxies did most of the work, with contributions from ultraviolet-bright spectra, a larger volume from the cosmological constant, merging, and a somewhat underestimated faint-end population. The detailed accounting still depends on galaxy formation models, but the puzzle stopped being a puzzle once the cosmic star formation history was measured.\
Shall we go on to Kaiser's work next?\
Let's go to Kaiser.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Stop 4: The theory becomes a tool (Kaiser and collaborators, 1992\'961995)
\f0\b0 \
Tyson's group had shown the signal existed. What the field lacked was a rigorous way to go from measured galaxy shapes to a mass distribution, and a trustworthy way to measure those shapes in the first place. Nick Kaiser, with his student Gordon Squires and with Tom Broadhurst, supplied both in a few years.\

\f1\b The context
\f0\b0 \
Kaiser was already well known in cosmology for two ideas: galaxy "biasing" (1984), explaining why clusters cluster more strongly than the underlying matter, and redshift-space distortions (1987), which use galaxy peculiar velocities to measure the matter density. Both reflect the same concern: galaxies are imperfect tracers of mass, so how do you get at the mass itself? Lensing was an obvious next step.\
The cosmological stakes were high. In the early 1990s the central question was the matter density, \uc0\u937 . Theorists favored \u937  = 1 on grounds of inflation and simplicity, while many observations suggested about 0.2 to 0.3. Cluster mass-to-light ratios were a key input, since multiplying the average M/L by the universe's total luminosity gives an estimate of \u937 . But cluster masses from galaxy velocities assumed dynamical equilibrium, and masses from ROSAT X-ray data assumed hydrostatic equilibrium of the hot gas. Lensing offered a measurement free of both assumptions, which is why a mass-mapping method mattered so much. COBE's 1992 detection of CMB fluctuations added pressure to test structure formation models against the actual mass distribution.\

\f1\b Kaiser (1992): cosmic shear as a power spectrum
\f0\b0 \
In "Weak Gravitational Lensing of Distant Galaxies" (ApJ, 1992), Kaiser worked out the statistics of the distortions produced by large-scale structure rather than by a single cluster. His key technical contribution was a Fourier-space version of the Limber approximation, which relates the angular power spectrum of the shear to the three-dimensional matter power spectrum weighted along the line of sight. Blandford, Saust, Brainerd & Villumsen (1991) and Miralda-Escud\'e9 (1991) had reached similar conclusions around the same time. Kaiser's formalism became the standard language for cosmic shear, which we'll return to at Stop 6.\

\f1\b Kaiser & Squires (1993): mapping the mass
\f0\b0 \
"Mapping the Dark Matter with Weak Gravitational Lensing" (ApJ, 1993) is probably the most influential paper of this stop. The insight is elegant. In the weak limit, both the convergence \uc0\u954  (the projected mass density, in units of a critical density) and the shear \u947  are second derivatives of a single function, the lensing potential \u968 :\
\uc0\u954  = \'bd(\u8706 \u8321 \'b2 + \u8706 \u8322 \'b2)\u968 , \u947 \u8321  = \'bd(\u8706 \u8321 \'b2 \u8722  \u8706 \u8322 \'b2)\u968 , \u947 \u8322  = \u8706 \u8321 \u8706 \u8322 \u968 \
In Fourier space the derivatives become multiplications, so you can write \uc0\u954  directly in terms of the measured shear:\
\uc0\u954 \u770 (\u8467 ) = [(\u8467 \u8321 \'b2 \u8722  \u8467 \u8322 \'b2) \u947 \u770 \u8321  + 2\u8467 \u8321 \u8467 \u8322  \u947 \u770 \u8322 ] / \u8467 \'b2\
Equivalently, in real space, the mass map is the shear field convolved with a kernel falling off as 1/\uc0\u952 \'b2. The average ellipticity of galaxies in each small patch estimates the local shear, and this inversion turns the pattern into a picture of the projected mass. Tyson's group had fitted models; Kaiser and Squires made it possible to see the dark matter's layout without assuming a shape for it.\
The paper also dealt with the practical consequences. Intrinsic galaxy ellipticities act as noise, so they derived the noise in the reconstructed map, set by the number of background galaxies per unit area. That formula told observers directly how deep they needed to go. They also noted a built-in check: the true shear field comes from a single potential, so a "rotated" component of the signal (now called the B-mode) should vanish. Finding one indicates systematic errors, a diagnostic that became central to later surveys.\

\f1\b The complications
\f0\b0 \
The original inversion had known weaknesses, and much of 1994\'961996 was spent fixing them. Kaiser, Seitz & Schneider, Bartelmann and others all contributed.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Finite fields.
\f0\b0  The Fourier inversion assumes shear is measured everywhere, but real images have edges. Fahlman, Kaiser, Squires & Woods (1994) introduced an "aperture densitometry" statistic that measures the mean mass inside a circle relative to a surrounding annulus, using only shear in the annulus. Applied to the cluster MS 1224+20, it gave a strikingly high mass-to-light ratio, around 800 in solar units, well above other estimates. Schneider (1996) later generalized the idea into the "aperture mass."\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reduced shear.
\f0\b0  What galaxies actually record isn't \uc0\u947  but g = \u947 /(1 \u8722  \u954 ), which differs noticeably near cluster cores. Kaiser (1995) and Seitz & Schneider (1995) developed nonlinear reconstructions to handle this.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The mass-sheet degeneracy.
\f0\b0  Adding a uniform sheet of mass (with a rescaling) leaves the reduced shear unchanged, a problem Falco, Gorenstein & Shapiro had first identified for strong lenses in 1985. Shape measurements alone therefore only determine mass up to a constant. Broadhurst, Taylor & Peacock (1995) proposed breaking it with magnification, which changes the number counts of background galaxies behind a cluster.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 KSB (1995): measuring the shapes
\f0\b0 \
The other half of the problem was the point spread function. Atmospheric seeing and telescope optics blur every galaxy, which makes images rounder and dilutes the shear. PSF anisotropy, from tracking errors or optical aberrations, stretches all images in a common direction, which can mimic a lensing signal outright. Since the lensing distortion is only a few percent, even small PSF errors are fatal.\
Kaiser, Squires & Broadhurst, in "A Method for Weak Lensing Observations" (ApJ, 1995), built a correction scheme around weighted second moments of each galaxy's light profile. The weighting (a Gaussian matched to the object's size) keeps sky noise from swamping the measurement. The core idea is to treat the PSF as a small perturbation and calculate how each galaxy's ellipticity responds to it, through a quantity they called the smear polarizability, and how it responds to a genuine shear, through the shear polarizability. Stars in the same image are point sources, so their measured shapes reveal the PSF anisotropy directly, and this can be interpolated to each galaxy's position and subtracted. Luppino & Kaiser (1997) then completed the method with a correction for the isotropic blurring that dilutes the signal.\
"KSB" became the workhorse of the field for more than a decade. It was used in most early cluster studies and in several of the 2000 cosmic shear detections. Its approximations eventually became a limitation, since the perturbative treatment isn't exact for realistic PSFs and galaxy profiles. The shape measurement challenges we'll meet at Stop 8 grew largely out of efforts to understand KSB's biases and replace it.\

\f1\b Why these papers matter
\f0\b0 \
Kaiser and collaborators turned weak lensing from a detection into a measurement. After 1995, an observer had a recipe: correct shapes with KSB, invert with Kaiser\'96Squires or a finite-field variant, check the B-mode, and read off a mass. Early applications, such as Squires and colleagues' studies of Abell 2218 and Abell 2163, generally found lensing masses consistent with X-ray estimates, which lent confidence to both. The same framework, scaled up from clusters to random patches of sky, is what made cosmic shear surveys possible.\
Would you like to look more closely at the mass-sheet degeneracy or the KSB method, or go on to Stop 5, galaxy\'96galaxy lensing?\
Explain the elegant insight of Kaiser 1993 in more detail\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The insight is that 
\f1\b shear and convergence are not independent quantities
\f0\b0 . Both come from the same underlying function, so measuring one tells you the other. The rest of the paper follows from that.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Step 1: Everything comes from one potential
\f0\b0 \
In the thin-lens picture, a light ray passing a mass distribution is deflected by an angle \uc0\u945 . For weak fields, that deflection is the gradient of a two-dimensional 
\f1\b lensing potential
\f0\b0  \uc0\u968 , which is the projected Newtonian potential of the lens, suitably scaled by the distances. The potential obeys a 2D Poisson equation:\
\uc0\u8711 \'b2\u968  = 2\u954 \
Here \uc0\u954  is the projected surface mass density divided by a critical density that depends on the lens and source distances. So far this looks like ordinary gravity squashed onto a plane.\

\f1\b Step 2: How images are distorted
\f0\b0 \
A small galaxy image is mapped from the source plane to the observed plane by the Jacobian matrix of the lens mapping. Since the deflection is a gradient, the Jacobian is built from the second derivatives of \uc0\u968 :\
A = [[1 \uc0\u8722  \u954  \u8722  \u947 \u8321 , \u8722 \u947 \u8322 ], [\u8722 \u947 \u8322 , 1 \u8722  \u954  + \u947 \u8321 ]]\
where\
\uc0\u954  = \'bd(\u8706 \u8321 \'b2\u968  + \u8706 \u8322 \'b2\u968 ), \u947 \u8321  = \'bd(\u8706 \u8321 \'b2\u968  \u8722  \u8706 \u8322 \'b2\u968 ), \u947 \u8322  = \u8706 \u8321 \u8706 \u8322 \u968 \
This splits the distortion into two parts, echoing Sachs's optical scalars from Stop 2. The trace, \uc0\u954 , magnifies images uniformly. The traceless part, the shear (\u947 \u8321 , \u947 \u8322 ), stretches them into ellipses. Shear has two components because an ellipse's orientation matters: \u947 \u8321  describes stretching along the x or y axes, and \u947 \u8322  describes stretching along the diagonals.\
The key observation is that \uc0\u968  has three second derivatives, \u8706 \u8321 \'b2\u968 , \u8706 \u8322 \'b2\u968  and \u8706 \u8321 \u8706 \u8322 \u968 , and \u954 , \u947 \u8321  and \u947 \u8322  are just three different combinations of them. They aren't three independent fields. They are three views of one field.\
The physical meaning is that the shear at a point is a 
\f1\b tidal
\f0\b0  effect produced by mass located 
\f2\i elsewhere
\f0\i0 . A lump of mass stretches the images of galaxies around it tangentially, which is exactly what Tyson saw. If you know the tidal field everywhere, you can work backward to where the mass is. This is like reconstructing the charges in electrostatics from the field they produce.\

\f1\b Step 3: In Fourier space it becomes almost trivial
\f0\b0 \
Taking Fourier transforms turns each derivative \uc0\u8706 \u11388  into a multiplication by i\u8467 \u11388 , where \u8467  is the 2D wavevector. The equations become algebraic:\
\uc0\u954 \u770  = \u8722 \'bd(\u8467 \u8321 \'b2 + \u8467 \u8322 \'b2) \u968 \u770 \u8232 \u947 \u770 \u8321  = \u8722 \'bd(\u8467 \u8321 \'b2 \u8722  \u8467 \u8322 \'b2) \u968 \u770 \u8232 \u947 \u770 \u8322  = \u8722 \u8467 \u8321 \u8467 \u8322  \u968 \u770 \
Now you can eliminate \uc0\u968 \u770  entirely. It helps to write the shear as a complex number, \u947  = \u947 \u8321  + i\u947 \u8322 , and to describe the wavevector by its length \u8467  and angle \u966 . Then:\
\uc0\u947 \u770 (\u8467 ) = e^(2i\u966 ) \u954 \u770 (\u8467 )\

\f1\b For each Fourier mode, the shear equals the convergence multiplied by a pure phase factor.
\f0\b0  The factor has magnitude one. It doesn't change the amplitude, only rotates it by twice the wavevector's angle. The "2" appears because shear is a spin-2 quantity: an ellipse looks the same after a 180\'b0 rotation.\
Inverting this is immediate:\
\uc0\u954 \u770 (\u8467 ) = e^(\u8722 2i\u966 ) \u947 \u770 (\u8467 )\
Taking the real part and writing it out gives the formula I quoted earlier:\
\uc0\u954 \u770  = [(\u8467 \u8321 \'b2 \u8722  \u8467 \u8322 \'b2) \u947 \u770 \u8321  + 2\u8467 \u8321 \u8467 \u8322  \u947 \u770 \u8322 ] / \u8467 \'b2\
Measure the shear field, Fourier transform it, rotate each mode's phase, transform back, and you have a map of the projected mass. You don't need a model, and you don't need to assume anything about equilibrium, symmetry or how the mass relates to light.\

\f1\b Step 4: The real-space picture
\f0\b0 \
The same operation in real space is a convolution:\
\uc0\u954 (\u952 ) = (1/\u960 ) \u8747  Re[D*(\u952  \u8722  \u952 \u8242 ) \u947 (\u952 \u8242 )] d\'b2\u952 \u8242 \
The kernel D falls off as 1/|\uc0\u952 |\'b2 and has a spin-2 angular pattern. In practice this means that to estimate the mass at a point, you add up the 
\f1\b tangential shear
\f0\b0  of galaxies around that point, weighting nearby galaxies more heavily. A mass concentration shows up as galaxies arranged in rings around it, and the kernel is tuned to detect that pattern. Tyson's "tangential alignment" signature is built directly into the inversion.\

\f1\b Step 5: From galaxies to a map
\f0\b0 \
Galaxies aren't round, so each measured ellipticity is the sum of the lensing shear and the galaxy's intrinsic shape:\
\uc0\u949  \u8776  \u947  + \u949 _intrinsic\
If intrinsic orientations are random, they average to zero, so the mean ellipticity in a small patch estimates the local shear. Kaiser and Squires replaced the integral with a sum over individual galaxies, with smoothing to keep the noise finite.\
Because the Fourier kernel has magnitude one, the noise works out simply. Intrinsic shapes produce white noise in the shear, and the inversion passes it straight through, so the noise in the mass map is also white. A map smoothed over a scale \uc0\u952 \u8347  has noise roughly\
\uc0\u963 _\u954 \'b2 \u8764  \u963 _\u949 \'b2 / (n \u952 \u8347 \'b2)\
where \uc0\u963 _\u949  is the scatter in intrinsic ellipticities (around 0.3 per component) and n is the number of background galaxies per unit area. This told observers exactly what they needed: more galaxies per square arcminute buy you finer or less noisy maps. It's a large part of why weak lensing drove telescopes toward deeper imaging and better seeing.\

\f1\b Step 6: A free consistency check
\f0\b0 \
The shear field has two components but is determined by one scalar function. The system is overdetermined, and that redundancy gives a test.\
Gravity produces only shear patterns that come from a potential, called 
\f1\b E-modes
\f0\b0  by analogy with electric fields, which are gradients. In the inversion, these end up in the real part of e^(\uc0\u8722 2i\u966 )\u947 \u770 . The imaginary part, the 
\f1\b B-mode
\f0\b0 , should be zero apart from noise. A shear pattern with a curl, like a magnetic field, can't come from lensing.\
A B-mode in the data therefore signals a problem: uncorrected PSF anisotropy, errors in the shape measurements, or other systematics. A useful trick is that rotating every galaxy by 45\'b0 swaps E and B, so it turns the real signal off. This check has since become standard in every cosmic shear survey.\

\f1\b Step 7: What the inversion cannot do
\f0\b0 \
The phase factor is undefined at \uc0\u8467  = 0. A perfectly uniform sheet of mass has constant \u954 , and all its second derivatives other than the trace vanish, so it produces 
\f1\b no shear at all
\f0\b0 . The inversion therefore recovers \uc0\u954  only up to an additive constant. This is the mass-sheet degeneracy in its simplest form. (With reduced shear it becomes a slightly more complicated transformation, but it stays an issue.)\
Two other assumptions caused trouble in practice. The Fourier method assumes shear is known over an infinite plane, so real images with edges produce artifacts near the borders. And galaxies actually measure the reduced shear \uc0\u947 /(1 \u8722  \u954 ), not \u947 , which matters near cluster cores. These were the problems the finite-field and nonlinear methods of 1994\'961996 addressed.\

\f1\b Why it's elegant
\f0\b0 \
The whole argument rests on one fact: lensing distortions come from a single potential. That turns a messy inverse problem into a phase rotation. The result is linear, model-free, and has the right noise behavior. It also comes with its own error check. For the first time, astronomers could produce an actual 
\f2\i image
\f0\i0  of dark matter, rather than fitting a guessed profile to a statistical signal.\
