\f1\b \cf0 Stop 5: Galaxy\'96galaxy lensing (1984\'962006)
\f0\b0 \
Cluster lensing weighs the biggest objects in the universe. Galaxy\'96galaxy lensing asks the same question of ordinary galaxies: how much dark matter surrounds a typical galaxy, and how far out does it extend?\

\f1\b The context: how big are dark halos?
\f0\b0 \
By the late 1970s, flat rotation curves had convinced most astronomers that spiral galaxies sit inside dark matter halos. But rotation curves run out of tracers. Beyond the edge of a galaxy's neutral hydrogen, typically a few tens of kiloparsecs, there is nothing left to measure, so the halo's full extent and total mass were unknown. A flat rotation curve means mass grows in proportion to radius, so whether a halo stops at 30 kpc or 300 kpc makes a tenfold difference in its mass. Other methods, such as the motions of satellite galaxies (Zaritsky and White in the early 1990s) and the dynamics of binary galaxies, pointed toward large halos but depended on small samples and assumptions about orbits.\
Lensing offered a direct probe at exactly those large radii. The problem is that a single galaxy is a very weak lens. Its shear on background galaxies at distances of tens of arcseconds is well under 1%, far smaller than the scatter in intrinsic shapes. You can never see it around one galaxy. The only option is to 
\f1\b stack
\f0\b0 : average the tangential alignment of background galaxies around many thousands of foreground galaxies.\

\f1\b Tyson, Valdes, Jarvis & Mills (1984): the first attempt
\f0\b0 \
In "Galaxy Mass Distribution from Gravitational Light Deflection" (ApJ Letters, 1984), Tyson's group tried exactly this, using digitized photographic plates from the 4-meter Mayall telescope covering tens of thousands of galaxies. They split the galaxies by brightness, treating brighter ones as foreground lenses and fainter ones as background sources, and looked for tangential alignment around the lenses.\
They found no signal. From the upper limit, they argued that galaxy halos must be smaller or less massive than extended-halo models suggested, which would have been an important result. Kovner and Milgrom (1987) reanalyzed the problem and argued that the limits were considerably weaker than claimed, partly because of assumptions about the source redshifts and the way lenses and sources overlap in distance. The consensus that emerged was that the experiment simply lacked the sensitivity: photographic plates, modest seeing, and uncertain redshifts could not reach the required precision. As I mentioned at Stop 3, this null result, along with the cosmic shear upper limits, pushed Tyson's group toward the stronger signal from clusters.\

\f1\b Brainerd, Blandford & Smail (1996): the first detection
\f0\b0 \
The first convincing detection came from Tereasa Brainerd, Roger Blandford, and Ian Smail in "Weak Gravitational Lensing by Galaxies" (ApJ, 1996). Their data came from deep CCD imaging on the Palomar 5-meter telescope, a single field but with much better image quality than the 1984 plates.\
Like Tyson, they had no redshifts, so they used magnitude as a proxy: galaxies in a brighter range were treated as lenses, and fainter ones as sources. They then measured the average tangential ellipticity of sources as a function of angular distance from the lenses, over scales of several to a few tens of arcseconds. They found a significant tangential alignment. Crucially, they also checked that the signal disappeared when the sources were rotated by 45\'b0, the same E/B trick from Kaiser and Squires.\
To interpret the signal, they modeled the lenses as isothermal halos with a cutoff radius and used simulations to account for the unknown redshift distributions. Their result was consistent with halos having circular velocities around 200 km/s, similar to the Milky Way, and extending to at least roughly 100 kpc, well beyond where rotation curves can reach. It was a modest-significance measurement from one field, but it established that the method worked and that halos are large.\

\f1\b Refinement (1996\'962004)
\f0\b0 \
Several groups quickly followed. Dell'Antonio & Tyson (1996) and Hudson and collaborators (1998) applied the method to the Hubble Deep Field, where HST's sharp images helped measure small faint galaxies, and Hudson's group used photometric redshifts to separate lenses and sources more reliably. Griffiths and collaborators used HST's Medium Deep Survey. On the theory side, Schneider & Rix (1997) developed a maximum-likelihood method that used each lens-source pair optimally rather than just binning.\
The major step came from wide-field surveys. Fischer and collaborators (2000) used commissioning data from the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, covering far more area than any earlier study, and detected the signal at high significance out to hundreds of kiloparsecs. They concluded that halos extend beyond about 260 kpc (with h scaling). McKay et al. (2001) added SDSS spectroscopic redshifts for the lenses, allowing the signal to be expressed in physical units and split by galaxy luminosity and type. Hoekstra and collaborators (2004) used the Red-sequence Cluster Survey to go further, fitting truncated halos and finding evidence that halos do have finite extents, as well as hints that they are flattened.\

\f1\b A change of perspective
\f0\b0 \
As the data improved, the interpretation shifted. Early studies modeled each lens as an isolated galaxy with its own halo. But on large scales, the stacked signal also includes mass from neighboring galaxies and the group or cluster a galaxy lives in. Guzik & Seljak (2001) and others interpreted galaxy\'96galaxy lensing through the 
\f1\b halo model
\f0\b0 , which separates the signal into a "one-halo" term from the galaxy's own halo and a "two-halo" term from surrounding structure.\
In this language, galaxy\'96galaxy lensing measures the cross-correlation between galaxies and mass, usually expressed as the excess surface density \uc0\u916 \u931 . That makes it a tool not just for halo sizes but for the 
\f1\b galaxy\'96halo connection
\f0\b0 : how galaxy luminosity, stellar mass, and color relate to the mass of the halo hosting them. Sheldon et al. (2004) and Mandelbaum et al. (2006), using large SDSS samples, measured halo masses as a function of these properties and found, among other things, that red galaxies sit in more massive halos than blue galaxies of the same stellar mass.\

\f1\b Why it matters
\f0\b0 \
Galaxy\'96galaxy lensing gave the first direct measurement of dark matter halos at the radii where they hold most of their mass, confirming that galaxies are embedded in large halos, as structure formation theory predicted. Its later role is arguably even more important. Galaxy clustering alone can't separate the matter density from how galaxies trace matter (Kaiser's bias). Combining clustering with galaxy\'96galaxy lensing and cosmic shear breaks that degeneracy. This "3\'d72-point" combination is the core analysis of modern surveys like DES and KiDS, which we'll reach at Stop 10.\
Next is Stop 6, the theoretical predictions for cosmic shear, which set the targets for the 2000 detections. Shall we continue?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Quote, 1 line\
\pard\pardeftab720\li800\fi-800\ri800\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Hoekstra and collaborators (2004) used the Red-sequence Cluster Survey to go further, fitting truncated halos and finding evidence that halos do have finite extents, as well as hints that they are flattened.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 What do you mean by flattened?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "Flattened" means the dark matter halo is 
\f1\b not spherical
\f0\b0 . It's squashed into an ellipsoid, so its projection on the sky looks elliptical rather than circular.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Why anyone expected this
\f0\b0 \
Simple models, including the isothermal spheres used by Brainerd, Blandford & Smail, treat halos as round for convenience. But cold dark matter simulations from the late 1980s onward (Frenk and collaborators, Dubinski & Carlberg, and others) consistently produced halos that are 
\f1\b triaxial
\f0\b0 , with three different axis lengths. That's a consequence of how they form: matter falls in preferentially along filaments, and mergers come from particular directions, so the collapse isn't the same in every direction. Halo shape therefore became a test of the dark matter model:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Standard cold dark matter predicts noticeably non-spherical halos.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Baryons cooling into the center tend to make the inner halo rounder, so the degree of flattening also reflects galaxy formation physics.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Self-interacting dark matter would make halos rounder, because collisions between particles isotropize their orbits.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Modified gravity theories like MOND, which have no dark halo at all, predict that the lensing signal far from a galaxy should become nearly circular, since the effective field approaches that of a point mass.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Before lensing, halo shapes were constrained only in a few special cases, such as polar ring galaxies, flaring gas disks, and X-ray gas in ellipticals. These probe inner regions of individual objects.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 How lensing measures it
\f0\b0 \
The trick, proposed around 2000 by Brainerd & Wright and by Natarajan & Refregier, is to use the 
\f1\b visible galaxy as a compass
\f0\b0 . You can't see a single halo's orientation, but if halos tend to line up with the light of the galaxy inside them, you can rotate every lens so its major axis points the same way, then stack.\
A flattened halo has more mass along its long axis, so background galaxies near that axis are sheared more strongly than those near the short axis. The measurement compares the tangential shear of sources lying close to the lenses' major axes with that of sources near the minor axes. A round halo gives equal signals. A flattened one aligned with the light gives a stronger signal along the major axis.\

\f1\b What Hoekstra, Yee & Gladders (2004) found
\f0\b0 \
Using about 45 square degrees from the Red-sequence Cluster Survey, they modeled each halo's ellipticity as a fraction f of the lens galaxy's observed ellipticity. Their best fit was f of roughly 0.77, meaning halos are somewhat rounder than the light but flattened and aligned with it. They excluded round halos at about the 99% level, which implies a typical projected axis ratio of about two-thirds. They presented it as evidence against MOND-like isotropic signals and in line with CDM expectations.\

\f1\b What happened next
\f0\b0 \
This result turned out to be harder to confirm than it first appeared, which is why I only said "hints." Mandelbaum et al. (2006), with a much larger SDSS sample, found no significant anisotropy overall, with only a weak hint for bright red galaxies. Later studies from CFHTLS, CFHTLenS, KiDS and others (Parker et al. 2007, van Uitert et al. 2012, Schrabback et al. 2015 and 2021) have generally found weak or null signals, especially for spiral galaxies.\
The likely reasons are informative:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The compass is unreliable.
\f0\b0  Simulations show that a galaxy's light is often misaligned with its halo, particularly for disk galaxies, whose spin may be tilted relative to the halo's long axis. Misalignment washes out the stacked signal even if individual halos are strongly flattened.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The signal is tiny.
\f0\b0  It's a small modulation of an already tiny shear, so it needs enormous samples.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Systematics mimic it.
\f0\b0  Residual PSF anisotropy, intrinsic alignments of sources near the lens, and contamination from neighboring galaxies can all create or cancel an azimuthal pattern.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The current picture is that halos around massive elliptical galaxies do appear flattened and roughly aligned with their light, while the situation for spirals remains unclear. Halo shape is still an active research topic.\
Shall we move on to Stop 6 and the cosmic shear predictions?\
Yes, let's move on to Step 6\
