\f1\b \cf0 Stop 6: Cosmic shear predictions (1991\'961999)
\f0\b0 \
Cosmic shear is the purest form of weak lensing. Instead of pointing at a cluster or stacking around galaxies, you look at a random patch of sky and measure the faint, coherent distortion produced by all the matter along the line of sight. This is Kristian and Sachs's 1966 idea made quantitative. During the 1990s theorists worked out how big the signal should be, what it depends on, and how to extract cosmology from it. Those predictions set the targets for the detections at Stop 7.\

\f1\b The context
\f0\b0 \
Two things made this the right moment. Tyson's cluster detection and the new CCD mosaic cameras suggested that percent-level distortions might finally be measurable over wide areas. And cosmology urgently needed a direct measure of the matter power spectrum.\
The problem was a degeneracy. COBE fixed the amplitude of fluctuations on very large scales, but the amplitude on cluster scales, usually written \uc0\u963 \u8328 , depended on the model. The abundance of galaxy clusters (White, Efstathiou & Frenk 1993) constrained only a combination, roughly \u963 \u8328  \u937 _m^0.5, so a low-density universe with strong clustering looked much like a dense universe with weak clustering. Galaxy surveys measured clustering of light, not mass, so they were tied up with bias. Lensing responds to mass directly, which made it an attractive way to settle the \u937  debate.\
Observers had already tried and failed. Valdes, Jarvis & Tyson (1983) and Mould, Blandford, Villumsen and collaborators (1994) found only upper limits. Villumsen (1995) reported a marginal signal from the same data, but it wasn't convincing. The theorists' job was partly to say how deep and wide a survey had to be to succeed.\

\f1\b The first predictions (1991)
\f0\b0 \
Three papers appeared almost together. Blandford, Saust, Brainerd & Villumsen (MNRAS 1991) and Miralda-Escud\'e9 (ApJ 1991) both calculated how large-scale structure distorts distant galaxy images, and Kaiser (ApJ 1992, submitted in 1991) followed with a Fourier-space treatment.\
Their shared conclusion was that the distortion is small but not hopelessly so. In plausible cold dark matter models, galaxy ellipticities should be correlated at roughly the percent level on scales of arcminutes, falling off at larger separations. Miralda-Escud\'e9 framed the key statistic as the 
\f1\b ellipticity correlation function
\f0\b0 , which measures how aligned pairs of galaxies are as a function of separation. He showed it is a weighted projection of the matter power spectrum. Blandford and collaborators emphasized that the signal reflects the mass on scales of a few megaparsecs, and they compared their predictions with the existing observational limits.\
Kaiser's version became the standard. Using the Limber approximation in Fourier space, he wrote the convergence power spectrum as an integral along the line of sight:\
P_\uc0\u954 (\u8467 ) = (9/4) \u937 _m\'b2 (H\u8320 /c)\u8308  \u8747  d\u967  [W(\u967 )/a(\u967 )]\'b2 P_\u948 (\u8467 /f_K(\u967 ); \u967 )\
Here P_\uc0\u948  is the three-dimensional matter power spectrum, \u967  is comoving distance, a is the scale factor, and W is a lensing efficiency set by the source redshift distribution. W peaks roughly halfway to the sources. By the Kaiser\'96Squires relation from Stop 4, the shear has the same power spectrum as \u954 , so measuring galaxy shapes measures P_\u954 .\
Two features of the formula explain much of what followed. The \uc0\u937 _m\'b2 prefactor and the \u963 \u8328 \'b2 in P_\u948  mean the signal depends mainly on a combination like \u963 \u8328  \u937 _m^\u945 . This is the same degeneracy as cluster counts, though with different weighting. Modern surveys quote it as 
\f1\b S\uc0\u8328  = \u963 \u8328  (\u937 _m/0.3)^0.5
\f0\b0 , which will matter at Stop 10. The kernel W also shows why source redshifts matter. Put the sources further away and the signal rises steeply.\

\f1\b Nonlinearity changes the picture (1996\'961997)
\f0\b0 \
The early calculations mostly used the linear power spectrum. But the arcminute scales where the signal is easiest to measure correspond to structures of a few hundred kiloparsecs to a few megaparsecs, where gravitational collapse is strongly nonlinear.\
Jain & Seljak (ApJ 1997), building on work by Villumsen (1996), used the new fitting formulas for the nonlinear power spectrum (Hamilton et al. 1991; Peacock & Dodds 1996). They found that nonlinear growth boosts the signal on arcminute scales by a large factor, making the rms shear there about 1\'962% rather than a fraction of a percent. That was good news for observers. It also meant that accurate predictions required modeling nonlinear clustering, and eventually the effects of baryons, a problem the field still wrestles with.\
Jain, Seljak & White (2000) then ran ray-tracing simulations through N-body cosmological volumes, tracing light rays through many lens planes. This tested the analytic approximations and captured the non-Gaussian statistics of the convergence field.\

\f1\b Breaking the degeneracy with higher-order statistics (1997)
\f0\b0 \
Bernardeau, van Waerbeke & Mellier (A&A 1997) offered a way around the \uc0\u963 \u8328 \'96\u937 _m degeneracy. Nonlinear gravity makes the convergence field non-Gaussian, with rare high peaks from clusters and broad shallow voids. The 
\f1\b skewness
\f0\b0  of \uc0\u954 , suitably normalized, is nearly independent of \u963 \u8328  but depends on \u937 _m, roughly as \u937 _m^\u8722 0.8 in perturbation theory. The variance measures the \u963 \u8328 \'96\u937 _m combination, and the skewness pins down \u937 _m on its own. This launched the long program of non-Gaussian lensing statistics, which now includes peak counts, Minkowski functionals, and machine-learning summaries.\

\f1\b Making it measurable (1996\'961998)
\f0\b0 \
Other papers turned the predictions into practical survey designs.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Aperture mass statistics.
\f0\b0  Schneider, van Waerbeke, Jain & Kruse (MNRAS 1998) proposed measuring the variance of the aperture mass across many randomly placed apertures. It acts as a narrow filter in \uc0\u8467 , making it a fairly clean probe of the power spectrum, and it separates naturally into E- and B-modes.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Survey requirements.
\f0\b0  Kaiser (ApJ 1998, "Weak Lensing and Cosmology") worked through the noise budget. Intrinsic shapes with \uc0\u963 _\u949  \u8776  0.3 mean that detecting a 1% shear takes on the order of a thousand galaxies per measurement, which pushes toward deep, wide imaging. He argued that a few square degrees could detect the signal and that larger surveys would constrain the power spectrum well. He also stressed that PSF anisotropy had to be controlled to a level well below the signal.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The full sky.
\f0\b0  Stebbins (1996) and Kamionkowski, Babul, Cress & Refregier (1998) extended the formalism to the curved sky and developed the E/B decomposition as a systematics test for wide surveys.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Tomography and parameter forecasts (1999)
\f0\b0 \
The last key step came from Wayne Hu and Max Tegmark. Hu & Tegmark (ApJ Letters 1999) applied Fisher matrix forecasting, already standard in CMB work, to lensing surveys. They showed that lensing combined with the CMB could tightly constrain \uc0\u937 _m, the cosmological constant, and neutrino masses.\
Hu (ApJ Letters 1999), "Power Spectrum Tomography with Weak Lensing," introduced 
\f1\b tomography
\f0\b0 . Instead of treating all sources together, you divide them into redshift bins using photometric redshifts and measure the lensing signal within and across bins. Because the kernel W shifts with source distance, the bins weigh structure at different epochs differently, so tomography recovers some of the information lost in projection. That gives access to the growth of structure over time, which later became central to measuring dark energy. Every major survey today uses tomography.\
These forecasts arrived just as the 1998 supernova results established an accelerating universe. Weak lensing quickly became one of the main proposed probes of dark energy, which shaped the planning of surveys over the next two decades.\

\f1\b Why these papers matter
\f0\b0 \
By 1999 theorists had provided everything needed: the expected amplitude (about 1% on arcminute scales), the right statistics (correlation functions, aperture masses, power spectra), a systematics test (the B-mode), a strategy for breaking degeneracies (higher-order statistics and tomography), and survey requirements. The detection itself was waiting only on wide-field cameras and careful PSF correction. In early 2000, four groups announced it within weeks of each other.\
Shall we go on to Stop 7 and those simultaneous detections, or look more closely at why the signal depends on the combination \uc0\u963 \u8328  \u937 _m^0.5?\
Lets go to stop 7!\
