\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Stop 7: The 2000 cosmic shear detections
\f0\b0 \
In March 2000, four independent groups posted papers within a few weeks of each other, each reporting that galaxy ellipticities in random patches of sky are correlated at the level the theorists had predicted. After more than thirty years of upper limits, cosmic shear had been detected.\

\f1\b Why it happened all at once
\f0\b0 \
The timing wasn't a coincidence. Three things came together in the late 1990s.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Wide-field CCD mosaics.
\f0\i0  Earlier CCDs covered only a few arcminutes, too small for a statistical signal that needs many independent patches. Cameras like the UH8K (8 CCDs) and then the CFH12K at the Canada\'96France\'96Hawaii Telescope, and the Big Throughput Camera on the CTIO 4-meter, could image a significant fraction of a square degree per pointing to the depths lensing needs.\

\f2\i A working shape-measurement method.
\f0\i0  KSB from Stop 4 had been tested for five years on clusters, where the signal is ten times stronger. Groups now understood its behavior well enough to push it to the percent level.\

\f2\i Clear targets.
\f0\i0  The Stop 6 predictions, especially after Jain & Seljak's nonlinear boost, said the signal should be about 1% on arcminute scales, and Kaiser's noise estimates said a square degree or so of deep imaging should be enough. Everyone with suitable data could see that the detection was within reach, which created a race.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The four papers
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Bacon, Refregier & Ellis
\f0\i0  (MNRAS 2000) used the William Herschel Telescope on La Palma, observing about a dozen separate fields. Their fields were small but well separated, which kept them independent. They measured the variance of the average shear in cells a few arcminutes across, correcting shapes with a version of KSB they had calibrated on simulated images. They found a signal clearly above the level expected from noise and systematics.\

\f2\i Kaiser, Wilson & Luppino
\f0\i0  used UH8K data from CFHT, originally taken for other purposes, covering several fields. Kaiser was in a sense completing his own program from 1992 and 1998. Their preprint reported a detection of shear correlations and examined the systematics carefully, though it circulated mainly as a preprint rather than becoming the most-cited of the four.\

\f2\i Van Waerbeke, Mellier and collaborators
\f0\i0  (A&A 2000), from the Paris group, used CFHT data from both the UH8K and the new CFH12K, covering around two square degrees and on the order of a hundred thousand galaxies. They measured the variance of the shear on scales from about one to several arcminutes and showed its decline with scale matched the predicted shape. This group went on to lead VIRMOS-DESCART and later CFHTLS, the first large dedicated surveys.\

\f2\i Wittman, Tyson, Kirkman, Dell'Antonio & Bernstein
\f0\i0  (Nature 2000) used the Big Throughput Camera on the Blanco telescope, imaging three fields of about half a square degree each. For Tyson this closed a loop that began with the 1983 upper limits. They measured the ellipticity correlation out to larger separations, around half a degree, and reported that their data disfavored the then "standard" cold dark matter model with \uc0\u937 _m = 1 and high normalization, while being consistent with low-density models.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 How they convinced people
\f0\b0 \
The fundamental worry was that a 1% signal could be produced entirely by imperfect PSF correction. The groups used several checks, which became standard practice:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 45\'b0 test.
\f0\b0  Rotating all galaxies by 45\'b0 converts the lensing E-mode into a B-mode. The rotated signal should vanish.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Star\'96galaxy correlations.
\f0\b0  If residual PSF anisotropy leaks into the galaxy shapes, the corrected galaxy ellipticities will correlate with the star ellipticities. The groups showed this cross-correlation was small compared with the signal.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Uncorrected versus corrected.
\f0\b0  Showing that the raw PSF anisotropy had a different amplitude and scale dependence from the final signal.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Independent fields.
\f0\b0  A real cosmological signal should be similar in unrelated directions, while telescope effects need not be.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The strongest argument, though, was the agreement itself. Four teams, on different telescopes, with different cameras, observing strategies and analysis codes, found signals of the same amplitude and consistent with the same theory. It's hard to imagine instrumental systematics conspiring to do that.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 What the results said about cosmology
\f0\b0 \
The measurements were far too noisy for precision, but the amplitude fit low-density models with \uc0\u963 \u8328  somewhere around 0.8 to 1, consistent with cluster abundance constraints. This arrived during a remarkable period. The supernova results of 1998\'9699 pointed to a cosmological constant, and in April 2000, BOOMERanG (and soon MAXIMA) showed from the CMB that the universe is spatially flat. Cosmic shear added an independent mass-based measurement that fit the emerging concordance model of \u937 _m \u8776  0.3 with a cosmological constant. That strengthened the case for dedicating major telescope time to lensing surveys.\

\f1\b The aftermath (2000\'962003)
\f0\b0 \
The immediate follow-up both consolidated and complicated the picture.\
Confirmations came quickly from the VLT (Maoli et al. 2001) and from HST (Rhodes, Refregier & Groth 2001), whose stable space-based PSF provided a check free of atmospheric effects. Surveys then grew to tens of square degrees. Hoekstra, Yee & Gladders (2002) used the Red-sequence Cluster Survey, and Jarvis and collaborators (2003) used about 75 square degrees from the CTIO survey.\
The B-mode test also started to bite. Van Waerbeke and collaborators, analyzing the larger VIRMOS-DESCART dataset, found a nonzero B-mode at some scales. Pen, van Waerbeke & Mellier (2002) developed cleaner E/B separation, and the residual was traced largely to imperfect PSF correction. It was a warning that KSB wasn't accurate enough for the next level of precision.\
Then the numbers began to scatter. Compilations of early results (Refregier 2003 is a useful review) showed \uc0\u963 \u8328  values ranging from roughly 0.7 to 1.0 for \u937 _m = 0.3, a spread larger than the stated errors. Some early lensing values sat above X-ray cluster estimates, which had drifted to around 0.7. There's a nice irony here: at Stop 10 we'll meet the modern "S\u8328  tension," where lensing tends to come out 
\f2\i lower
\f0\i0  than the CMB prediction. In the early 2000s the disagreement ran the other way. Much of that early spread came from shape-measurement biases, uncertain source redshift distributions, and the use of approximate nonlinear power spectra.\

\f1\b Why this stop matters
\f0\b0 \
The 2000 papers completed the arc from Kristian and Sachs: coherent distortions by large-scale structure are real, measurable, and consistent with the cosmological model. But the aftermath showed that the precision needed for cosmology would require solving problems that had been manageable at the detection level. Shape measurement needed to be accurate to a fraction of a percent, redshift distributions needed calibration, and astrophysical contaminants had to be understood. That's the subject of Stop 8, where intrinsic alignments and the shape measurement challenges enter the story.\
Shall we go on to Stop 8, or look more closely at any of the four detection papers first?\
Continue!\
