\f1\b \cf0 Stop 8: Systematics come of age (2001\'962010)
\f0\b0 \
After 2000 the question changed from "is it there?" to "how precisely can we measure it?" The answer depended less on statistics than on systematics, meaning effects that mimic or distort the lensing signal. This decade identified the main ones and built the tools for dealing with them. Two stand out: intrinsic alignments of galaxies, and biases in shape measurement.\

\f1\b The context: dark energy raises the stakes
\f0\b0 \
The accelerating universe changed weak lensing's role. Tomography (Hu 1999) meant lensing could track the growth of structure over cosmic time, which depends on dark energy in a way that distance measurements alone don't capture. The Dark Energy Task Force report (Albrecht et al. 2006) ranked weak lensing among the most powerful probes of dark energy, and ambitious surveys were proposed: the Dark Energy Survey, LSST, the space missions that became Euclid, and what is now the Roman Space Telescope.\
The catch was precision. The 2000 detections tolerated errors of 10% or so. Forecasts for the new surveys showed that to avoid being limited by systematics, shear measurements would need to be accurate to about 0.1%, and photometric redshift distributions would need their mean known to a few parts in a thousand (Huterer et al. 2006; Ma, Hu & Huterer 2006; Amara & Refregier 2008). That's a factor of a hundred improvement, and it set the agenda for the decade.\

\f1\b Intrinsic alignments: the assumption that galaxies are randomly oriented
\f0\b0 \
Every weak lensing measurement assumes that intrinsic galaxy shapes average to zero, so that any coherent alignment comes from lensing. But galaxies form in the same tidal fields that produce lensing, and there are physical reasons to expect them to be aligned with their surroundings. Elliptical galaxies may be stretched along the local tidal field, and spiral galaxies may acquire spins aligned with it through tidal torques.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 The first worry (2000\'962001).
\f0\i0  Several papers raised this around the time of the detections: Heavens, Refregier & Heymans (2000) and Croft & Metzler (2000) used simulations, while Catelan, Kamionkowski & Blandford (2001) introduced the 
\f1\b linear alignment model
\f0\b0 , in which a galaxy's intrinsic ellipticity is proportional to the tidal field when it formed. Crittenden, Natarajan, Pen & Theuns (2001) treated spirals using tidal torque theory. Estimates of the contamination varied widely, from negligible to a sizable fraction of the signal.\
These papers focused on what is now called the 
\f1\b II term
\f0\b0 , correlations between the intrinsic shapes of galaxies that are physically close to each other. The problem seemed manageable. Since II only affects pairs at similar distances, King & Schneider (2002) and Heymans & Heavens (2003) showed that with photometric redshifts you can simply down-weight or remove such pairs.\

\f2\i Hirata & Seljak (2004): the problem that can't be removed.
\f0\i0  Chris Hirata and Uro\'9a Seljak, in "Intrinsic alignment\'96lensing interference as a contaminant of cosmic shear" (Physical Review D, 2004), identified a subtler effect. Consider a massive structure at some distance. It tidally aligns the galaxies near it, and it also lenses galaxies far behind it. The two effects are correlated, because they come from the same mass. This is the 
\f1\b GI term
\f0\b0 , a correlation between the 
\f2\i gravitational
\f0\i0  shear of a background galaxy and the 
\f2\i intrinsic
\f0\i0  shape of a foreground one.\
Its sign is the crucial part. In the linear alignment model, a foreground galaxy is stretched radially toward the mass concentration, while the lensed background galaxy is stretched tangentially around it. The GI term is therefore negative, and it partly 
\f2\i cancels
\f0\i0  the lensing signal. Worse, it comes from pairs at very different distances, so removing physically close pairs does nothing. Hirata and Seljak estimated it could bias the signal by up to tens of percent for plausible alignment amplitudes. A cosmic shear survey unaware of it would underestimate \uc0\u963 \u8328 .\

\f2\i Detecting and modeling it (2006\'962011).
\f0\i0  Mandelbaum et al. (2006) and Hirata et al. (2007) measured the correlation between galaxy positions and shapes in SDSS and found a clear alignment signal for luminous red galaxies, with no detection for blue galaxies. Bridle & King (2007) modified the linear model to use the nonlinear matter power spectrum. This 
\f1\b nonlinear alignment model
\f0\b0  is crude but became the default in nearly every survey analysis. Joachimi et al. (2011) measured the alignment amplitude's dependence on luminosity and redshift using the MegaZ-LRG sample.\
The field settled on two strategies. One is to include an alignment model with free parameters and marginalize over them, which costs statistical power but protects against bias. The other is to exploit the different redshift dependence of lensing and alignments to separate them, the "nulling" approach of Joachimi & Schneider (2008). Intrinsic alignments remain one of the leading theoretical systematics, and the S\uc0\u8328  tension discussion at Stop 10 involves them directly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Shape measurement: can we measure a 0.1% shear?
\f0\b0 \
The residual B-modes of the early 2000s showed that KSB had limits. KSB is a perturbative approximation, and several things break it at high precision: realistic PSFs aren't simple, galaxy profiles aren't Gaussian, and faint galaxies are noisy. Various alternatives appeared, including shapelets, which decompose galaxy images into a set of basis functions (Refregier 2003; Bernstein & Jarvis 2002), and fitting methods like im2shape (Bridle et al. 2002). But nobody knew how accurate any method really was, because real data don't come with the true shear attached.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 STEP: testing on simulated images.
\f0\i0  The Shear TEsting Programme answered this with blind tests. Simulated images with a known applied shear were distributed, and groups analyzed them without knowing the answer.\
STEP1 (Heymans et al. 2006) used simple ground-based simulations and compared about a dozen methods. It introduced the parametrization used ever since, relating measured to true shear by\
\uc0\u947 _measured = (1 + m) \u947 _true + c\
where m is the multiplicative bias and c the additive bias. The results were sobering. Many methods, including several KSB implementations, had multiplicative biases of a few percent to over 10%, and different implementations of the "same" method disagreed. A few methods reached about 2%, showing that better was possible.\
STEP2 (Massey et al. 2007) used more realistic galaxy shapes, built from shapelet models of real HST galaxies, and a wider range of PSFs. The best methods reached roughly 1% accuracy, but biases depended on galaxy size, magnitude and PSF shape. Averaging to zero was not guaranteed.\

\f2\i GREAT08: bringing in outsiders.
\f0\i0  The GRavitational lEnsing Accuracy Testing challenge (Bridle et al. 2009, 2010) took a new approach. It was designed as an open competition, published partly in the 
\f2\i Annals of Applied Statistics
\f0\i0 , to attract statisticians and computer scientists. It simplified the problem to its core (known PSF, simple galaxies, large noise) and asked entrants to measure the shear. Several new approaches performed well, and the challenge clarified what the fundamental difficulties were. GREAT10 (Kitching et al. 2012) and GREAT3 (Mandelbaum et al. 2015) followed with increasingly realistic tests.\

\f2\i What the challenges revealed.
\f0\i0  Two biases turned out to be fundamental rather than just imperfections in particular codes.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Noise bias.
\f0\b0  Ellipticity is a nonlinear function of pixel values, so even an unbiased image produces a biased shape estimate when noise is present. Refregier et al. (2012), Melchior & Viola (2012) and Kacprzak et al. (2012) quantified this. It's worst for the faint galaxies that carry much of the signal.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Model bias.
\f0\b0  Methods that fit a galaxy model get biased shapes when real galaxies don't match the model, for example galaxies with bulges, spiral arms or clumps.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 \strokec2 New methods.
\f0\i0  The lensfit method (Miller et al. 2007; Kitching et al. 2008) used Bayesian model fitting, with priors on galaxy properties drawn from data, to estimate shears for faint galaxies. It became the method for CFHTLenS and later KiDS. The general conclusion of the decade was that no method is exactly unbiased, so every survey must calibrate its residual bias with image simulations that closely match the real data. In the following decade, metacalibration (Huff & Mandelbaum 2017; Sheldon & Huff 2017) introduced a different idea: measuring the response of shapes to shear directly from the data by artificially shearing the observed images. DES adopted it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Other systematics identified in this period
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Photometric redshifts.
\f0\b0  Tomography needs redshifts for millions of galaxies, which only multi-band photometry can provide. Catastrophic outliers, where a galaxy's colors are consistent with a very different redshift, can bias results significantly. Calibrating redshift distributions became as important as calibrating shapes.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Baryonic physics.
\f0\b0  Predictions came from dark-matter-only simulations, but gas physics redistributes matter on small scales. White (2004) and Zhan & Knox (2004) flagged this. Van Daalen et al. (2011), using the OWLS simulations, showed that feedback from active galactic nuclei suppresses the matter power spectrum by tens of percent on small scales, a large effect for lensing. This remains a major modeling uncertainty.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Survey data quality.
\f0\b0  The early CFHTLS Wide analyses (Hoekstra et al. 2006; Fu et al. 2008) set tighter constraints than before, but later checks revealed residual systematics in some fields. This fed directly into the complete reanalysis that became CFHTLenS.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Why this stop matters
\f0\b0 \
By 2010 the field had a much more realistic picture of what precision lensing requires. Intrinsic alignments showed that astrophysics could contaminate the signal in ways that looked like cosmology. The shear challenges showed that shape measurement is a hard statistical problem that needs external calibration. And the whole enterprise had developed a culture of blind tests, image simulations, null tests and explicit error budgets. When the large surveys of the 2010s began, they also adopted 
\f1\b blinding
\f0\b0  of their cosmological results, precisely because the systematics had become subtle enough that analysts could unconsciously tune them toward an expected answer.\
Next is Stop 9, the Bullet Cluster and the COSMOS 3D dark matter map, which happened in parallel with all this. Want to go there next, or dig deeper into how the GI term works?\
