\f1\b \cf0 Stop 9: Dark matter made visible (2004\'962007)
\f0\b0 \
While the survey community was working through systematics, weak lensing produced its most famous results through a different route: mass maps of individual systems that showed dark matter clearly. Two stand out: the Bullet Cluster and the COSMOS three-dimensional map.\

\f1\b The context: was dark matter really settled?
\f0\b0 \
By the early 2000s most cosmologists accepted dark matter, but the evidence was indirect. Rotation curves, cluster dynamics, X-ray gas and lensing masses all showed that visible matter couldn't account for gravity 
\f2\i if
\f0\i0  general relativity and Newtonian gravity hold on those scales. A minority argued that gravity itself was the thing to change. Milgrom's Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND, 1983) explained galaxy rotation curves remarkably well with no dark matter, often better than dark matter models did at the time.\
For years MOND couldn't be tested with lensing, because it was a non-relativistic theory and said nothing about light. That changed in 2004 when Bekenstein published TeVeS, a relativistic version of MOND. Now both sides made lensing predictions, and the question sharpened: is there any system where the mass seen by lensing is clearly 
\f2\i somewhere else
\f0\i0  from the visible matter? In a modified-gravity universe without dark matter, the gravitational field should follow the baryons. With dark matter, the two can separate.\

\f1\b The Bullet Cluster (1E 0657\'96558)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 The system.
\f0\i0  This cluster at redshift 0.296 was identified in X-rays in the mid-1990s. Chandra observations by Markevitch and collaborators (2002) revealed what makes it special: it is two clusters that passed through each other roughly 150 million years ago, almost exactly in the plane of the sky. The smaller one's hot gas forms a bullet-shaped cloud, with a sharp bow shock in front of it indicating supersonic motion (Mach number around 3).\

\f2\i Why the collision separates things.
\f0\i0  A cluster contains three components that behave differently in a collision:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Galaxies
\f0\b0  are tiny compared to the space between them, so they pass through each other essentially without colliding.\
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Hot gas
\f0\b0  is a fluid. The two gas clouds collide, are slowed by ram pressure, and lag behind the galaxies. Crucially, this gas contains most of the ordinary matter in a cluster, several times more than the stars in galaxies.\
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Dark matter
\f0\b0 , if it is collisionless as standard theory assumes, should pass through like the galaxies.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 So after the collision, the bulk of the ordinary matter sits in the middle, between two groups of galaxies. If gravity follows ordinary matter, lensing should peak on the gas. If dark matter dominates, lensing should peak on the galaxies.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 The papers.
\f0\i0  Clowe, Gonzalez & Markevitch (ApJ 2004) produced the first weak lensing map, from ground-based imaging, and found the mass peaks offset from the gas at about 3.4\uc0\u963  significance. Clowe, Brada\u269 , Gonzalez, Markevitch, Randall, Jones & Zaritsky (ApJ Letters 2006), "A Direct Empirical Proof of the Existence of Dark Matter," combined deeper imaging from Magellan, the ESO Wide Field Imager and HST's ACS. They found that the two lensing peaks coincide with the galaxy concentrations and are offset from the X-ray gas peaks at 8\u963 . A companion paper by Brada\u269  and collaborators (2006) combined weak and strong lensing and reached the same conclusion with a more detailed map.\
The argument was designed to work even without assuming general relativity. In any gravity theory where the field is sourced by the visible matter, the lensing should follow the gas, since that's where most of the baryons are. It doesn't. The press release image, with the X-ray gas in pink and lensing mass in blue, became one of the most widely reproduced images in astronomy.\

\f2\i Constraining dark matter physics.
\f0\i0  The system also tests whether dark matter really is collisionless. If dark matter particles scattered off each other, the dark matter halos would be slowed and offset from the galaxies, and the small cluster would lose mass. Markevitch et al. (2004) used the observed alignment to set an upper limit on the self-interaction cross-section per unit mass of roughly 1 cm\'b2/g, and Randall et al. (2008) refined this to about 1.25 cm\'b2/g using simulations. This is still one of the standard astrophysical constraints on self-interacting dark matter.\

\f2\i Responses and complications.
\f0\i0  MOND advocates responded that the result doesn't rule out modified gravity plus 
\f2\i some
\f0\i0  unseen matter. Angus and collaborators (2006\'962007) argued that MOND could fit the Bullet Cluster if clusters contain substantial dark matter in the form of massive neutrinos around 2 eV. Many found this less appealing, since it reintroduces dark matter while keeping modified gravity, and later neutrino mass limits have made it harder to sustain. There was also a debate about the collision speed. The shock speed of roughly 4,700 km/s seemed high for \uc0\u923 CDM, but simulations (Springel & Farrar 2007) showed that the shock moves faster than the dark matter halo, which relieved much of the tension.\
Other merging clusters followed. MACS J0025.4\'961222 (Brada\uc0\u269  et al. 2008) showed the same separation. Abell 520, the "Train Wreck," was more puzzling: Mahdavi et al. (2007) and Jee et al. (2012) found a mass concentration coinciding with the gas and few galaxies, which seemed to contradict the Bullet Cluster, though Clowe et al. (2012) disputed this with a different analysis. Harvey et al. (2015) stacked 72 collisions in 30 systems and tightened the self-interaction limit to below about 0.5 cm\'b2/g, although later work argued their measured offsets are subject to modeling subtleties.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 COSMOS: a three-dimensional map of dark matter
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 The survey.
\f0\i0  The Cosmic Evolution Survey (COSMOS), led by Nick Scoville, imaged a 2-square-degree field with HST's ACS in about 590 orbits, the largest contiguous HST survey at the time. It was designed for galaxy evolution, with deep multi-wavelength data from X-ray to radio and photometric redshifts in dozens of bands. For lensing, the combination was ideal: space-based image quality means a stable PSF and a high density of resolved background galaxies, while the multi-band photometry gives accurate redshifts.\

\f2\i The map.
\f0\i0  Massey, Rhodes, Ellis, Scoville and collaborators (Nature 2007), "Dark matter maps reveal cosmic scaffolding," used about half a million galaxies. The key innovation was using photometric redshifts to go beyond a projected map. The lensing kernel from Stop 6 depends on source distance, so by splitting the sources into redshift slices and comparing the shear signal across them, they could estimate 
\f2\i where along the line of sight
\f0\i0  the mass lies. This is tomography applied to mapping rather than power spectra, building on ideas by Taylor (2001) and Hu & Keeton (2002).\
The resulting map extended to redshift about 1. It showed a network of clumps and filaments, and the dark matter generally coincided with concentrations of galaxies and with X-ray emitting gas found by XMM-Newton. It was the first time the "cosmic web" predicted by simulations had been mapped in dark matter rather than inferred from galaxies. The map also traced how structure became more clumpy over time, as expected from gravitational growth.\
Honest caveats apply. The resolution in the radial direction is poor, since the lensing kernel is broad, so the "3D" structure is smeared along the line of sight. The map is also noisy, and individual features at modest significance need to be treated with caution. Still, it demonstrated the potential of combining space-based shapes with good photometric redshifts, which is the design principle behind Euclid and Roman.\

\f2\i Other COSMOS lensing results.
\f0\i0  Massey et al. (2007, ApJS) and Leauthaud et al. (2007) described the shape catalog, and Schrabback et al. (2010) used COSMOS for cosmic shear tomography, finding evidence for accelerated expansion from lensing alone at modest significance. Leauthaud et al. (2010, 2012) used the field for galaxy\'96galaxy lensing and group studies, connecting back to the galaxy\'96halo relation from Stop 5.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 A related thread: finding clusters by mass
\f0\b0 \
Around the same time, lensing was used to 
\f2\i discover
\f0\i0  clusters rather than just weigh known ones. Wittman et al. (2001) found the first shear-selected cluster in the Deep Lens Survey, and Wittman et al. (2006) and Miyazaki et al. (2002, 2007) with Subaru produced larger samples. Selecting clusters by mass avoids the biases of selecting them by light or X-rays. However, projection effects from unrelated structures along the line of sight produce false peaks, a problem Hamana, White and others analyzed in detail. Peak counts later became a cosmological statistic in their own right, linking to the non-Gaussian ideas from Bernardeau and collaborators at Stop 6.\

\f1\b A cautionary tale: the CL0024 ring
\f0\b0 \
Jee et al. (2007) reported a ring-shaped dark matter structure in the cluster Cl 0024+17, interpreted as a ripple from a collision along the line of sight. It received wide publicity. Critics pointed out that such a feature could arise as an artifact of the reconstruction, particularly from how the PSF and the mass-sheet degeneracy were handled, and later analyses did not confirm it convincingly. It's a useful reminder that mass maps are noisy reconstructions, and striking features need independent confirmation.\

\f1\b Why this stop matters
\f0\b0 \
The Bullet Cluster turned weak lensing into direct physical evidence for dark matter, independent of assumptions about gravity's form, and gave the field its public face. COSMOS showed that lensing could map the cosmic web in three dimensions, validating the approach that the large surveys would take. Both results relied on everything from earlier stops: Kaiser\'96Squires inversion, careful PSF correction, and, for COSMOS, photometric redshifts and tomography.\
That brings us to the final stop, the large surveys of the 2010s and 2020s and the S\uc0\u8328  tension. Ready to go on?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
