\f1\b \cf0 Stacking many mergers (2015)
\f0\b0 \
Harvey, Massey, Kitching, Taylor and Tittley (Science 2015) used 72 subcluster collisions in 30 systems, observed with HST and Chandra. They measured the offsets between each subcluster's gas, galaxies and lensing mass, and found that the dark matter stays with the galaxies. They converted this into \uc0\u963 /m below about 0.47 cm\'b2/g, which was widely reported as the new benchmark.\

\f1\b The pushback (2017\'962018)
\f0\b0 \
That limit relied on a simple model of how a drag force would shift dark matter relative to galaxies. Later work showed the picture is more complicated.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Robertson, Massey and Eke (2017) ran simulations of Bullet-like collisions with self-interacting dark matter. They found that the galaxies, which are bound to the dark matter, get dragged along with it, so the offset between them stays small even for sizable cross-sections. The simple model overstated how visible the offset should be, and they concluded the Bullet Cluster constraints had been too strong.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Kim, Peter and Wittman (2017) similarly found that offsets are small and depend on when in the merger you observe them.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wittman, Golovich and Dawson (2018) reanalyzed the Harvey sample with more careful merger geometry and found the data only support a limit around 2 cm\'b2/g, several times weaker than the 2015 claim.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 So merging clusters today give a limit of order 1 cm\'b2/g, with real modeling uncertainty. The Bullet Cluster is still a clean qualitative demonstration of collisionless behavior, but it's no longer the strongest quantitative constraint.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Where the tighter limits come from now
\f0\b0 \
The strongest cluster-scale constraints now come from the 
\f1\b density profiles of relaxed clusters
\f0\b0 . Self-interactions carry heat inward and create constant-density cores, so the absence of large cores sets a limit. Analyses combining strong and weak lensing with stellar kinematics of the central galaxy (for example Kaplinghat, Tulin and Yu 2016, and Andrade and collaborators 2022) find limits of roughly 0.1 cm\'b2/g at the collision speeds typical of clusters, around 1,000\'961,500 km/s.\

\f1\b Velocity dependence complicates the comparison
\f0\b0 \
Many particle physics models of self-interaction predict a cross-section that falls with collision speed. Dwarf galaxies, with speeds of tens of km/s, may actually 
\f2\i favor
\f0\i0  cross-sections of a few cm\'b2/g to help explain their cored profiles, while clusters require much smaller values. So modern limits are usually quoted at a particular velocity, and the combination of dwarf, galaxy, group and cluster constraints maps out the velocity dependence rather than yielding one number.\
This is an active area, so there may be newer results from Euclid or JWST-era lensing that I'm not aware of. I can search for the latest constraints if you'd like. Otherwise, shall we go on to Stop 10?\
