\f1\b \cf0 Stop 10: The survey era and the S\uc0\u8328  tension (2008\'96today)
\f0\b0 \
This is where the history runs into the present, so I checked the latest results. The main story of this decade is the "S\uc0\u8328  tension": weak lensing surveys kept finding a less clumpy universe than the CMB predicts. That tension has recently eased considerably.\

\f1\b The context: Stage III surveys
\f0\b0 \
The Dark Energy Task Force sorted experiments into "stages." Stage III covered the ground-based surveys of the 2010s, each covering hundreds to thousands of square degrees. They were designed around the lessons from Stop 8. Shapes had to be calibrated with image simulations, redshift distributions needed careful calibration, and intrinsic alignments and baryonic effects had to be modeled. Results were blinded until the analysis was frozen.\

\f1\b CFHTLenS (2012\'962013): the template
\f0\b0 \
The CFHT Legacy Survey Wide field covered about 154 square degrees. Its first analyses had run into the residual systematics I mentioned earlier. The CFHTLenS team, led by Catherine Heymans and Ludovic van Waerbeke, reprocessed everything from the raw pixels: new PSF modeling, lensfit shapes, and photometric redshifts (Heymans et al. 2012; Erben et al. 2013). They tested each pointing for systematics and threw out about a quarter of them. Kilbinger et al. (2013) and Heymans et al. (2013), which did 6-bin tomography with intrinsic alignments included, produced the first cosmic shear constraints that were broadly trusted. The approach itself became the model for later surveys: reprocess from pixels, run explicit null tests, and blind the results.\

\f1\b The tension appears (2013\'962017)
\f0\b0 \
Planck's 2013 and 2015 results fixed the parameters of \uc0\u923 CDM with great precision. Extrapolated forward, they predicted a value of S\u8328  = \u963 \u8328 (\u937 _m/0.3)^0.5 of about 0.83. CFHTLenS came out lower. Several other low-redshift probes, such as Planck's own Sunyaev\'96Zel'dovich cluster counts, also sat below the prediction. MacCrann et al. (2015) quantified this and found a modest discrepancy that couldn't easily be fixed by extending the model, for example with massive neutrinos.\
The newer surveys then went in the same direction. KiDS-450 (Hildebrandt et al. 2017), from the Kilo-Degree Survey on ESO's VLT Survey Telescope, reported a roughly 2.3\uc0\u963  difference from Planck. KiDS was designed for lensing, and its nine-band optical plus near-infrared photometry gave it unusually good photometric redshifts.\

\f1\b The three big surveys (2018\'962023)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 DES
\f0\i0  used the Dark Energy Camera on the same Blanco telescope that Wittman and Tyson used in 2000. It introduced the 
\f1\b 3\'d72-point
\f0\b0  analysis from Stop 5, which combines cosmic shear, galaxy clustering, and galaxy\'96galaxy lensing. It also introduced metacalibration shapes. The Y1 (2018) and Y3 (2022) analyses were the headline results. For Y3 cosmic shear, the blind analysis gave S\uc0\u8328  = 0.759 \'b1 0.024 in \u923 CDM, a 2.3\u963  difference from Planck 2018 that the team did not consider statistically significant. The Y3 3\'d72pt result was about 0.776. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.darkenergysurvey.org/des-year-3-cosmology-results-papers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Dark Energy Survey}}\

\f2\i KiDS-1000
\f0\i0  (Asgari et al. 2021; Heymans et al. 2021) found similar values. With an improved redshift calibration using self-organizing maps, it found S\uc0\u8328  = 0.755 (+0.019/\u8722 0.021), a 3.2\u963  tension with Planck. That was the peak of the tension. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kids.strw.leidenuniv.nl/sciencedata.php"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Leidenuniv}}\

\f2\i Hyper Suprime-Cam (HSC)
\f0\i0  on the 8-meter Subaru telescope covers less area but goes much deeper. Its Y1 (Hikage et al. 2019; Hamana et al. 2020) and Y3 (2023) analyses also landed around 0.77.\
Then a joint DES Y3 + KiDS-1000 reanalysis (2023) harmonized the modeling choices and found S\uc0\u8328  \u8776  0.79, about 1.7\u963  below Planck. That already hinted that some of the discrepancy came from analysis choices.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 What could explain it?
\f0\b0 \
The debate focused on four possibilities, all of which link back to earlier stops.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A statistical fluctuation.
\f0\b0  Each result was only 2\'963\uc0\u963  from Planck, but they all pointed the same way.\
\ls14\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Systematics.
\f0\b0  The candidates were intrinsic alignments (Stop 8), photometric redshift errors (echoing the blue fog of Stop 3), and residual shear bias.\
\ls14\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Baryonic feedback.
\f0\b0  Amon & Efstathiou (2022) argued that stronger small-scale suppression of the matter power spectrum than standard models assume could reconcile lensing with Planck without new physics. This connects to van Daalen's 2011 result and is still an active debate.\
\ls14\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 New physics.
\f0\b0  Candidates included interacting dark matter, dark energy that affects the growth of structure, and modified gravity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 The recent turn (2025\'962026)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 KiDS-Legacy
\f0\i0  (Wright et al. 2025) analyzed the final survey. It covers 1,347 square degrees of nine-band imaging, and better redshift calibration and multi-band image simulations let the team extend the source sample to photometric redshift 2. It found S\uc0\u8328  = 0.81 (+0.016/\u8722 0.021), agreeing with Planck to within 0.73\u963 . The authors were open about how big a shift this was. Compared with the most similar earlier KiDS analysis, the tension with Planck dropped by 2.2\u963 , a change the team had accepted before unblinding but still found surprising. A companion paper found that KiDS-Legacy combined with DES Y3 cosmic shear, supernovae, and DESI BAO gives S\u8328  = 0.814 (+0.011/\u8722 0.012), consistent with Planck. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa54908-25/aa54908-25.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 KiDS-Legacy: Cosmological constraints from cosmic shear with the complete Kilo-Degree Survey | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A) +3}}\

\f2\i DES Y6
\f0\i0 , released in January 2026, is the final DES result. Its 3\'d72pt analysis covers about 5,000 square degrees, using 140 million source galaxy shapes and 9 million lens galaxies. It finds S\uc0\u8328  = 0.789 \'b1 0.012 and \u937 _m = 0.333, a 1.8\u963  difference from a combination of Planck 2018, ACT-DR6, and SPT-3G DR1 CMB data. The shape measurements show how far Stop 8's program has come. With the new metadetection method, the team found no detectable multiplicative bias at roughly the half-percent level, which is close to the 0.1% goal set around 2006. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2026arXiv260114559D/abstract"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Dark Energy Survey Year 6 Results: Cosmological Constraints from Galaxy Clustering and Weak Lensing - ADS +2}}\
So the current picture is that weak lensing still tends to come out slightly below the CMB, but the gap is now within about 2\uc0\u963 . The field is moving away from "tension" language and toward asking whether small remaining offsets reflect baryonic physics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Stage IV: the next chapter has just begun
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Euclid
\f0\i0 , the ESA mission, is essentially COSMOS scaled up to a third of the sky. It launched on 1 July 2023, with quick releases in March 2025 and June 2026. In November 2026, the mission will release raw and calibrated data over about 1,900 square degrees ("DR1-Foundation"), and the full DR1 follows in mid-2027. Its first cosmic shear cosmology should follow from those data. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.euclid-ec.org/public/timeline/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Timeline \'96 Euclid Consortium +2}}\

\f2\i Roman
\f0\i0  is NASA's wide-field infrared telescope, descended from the dark-energy mission concepts of the 2000s. It launched on a Falcon Heavy on 30 August 2026, just over two weeks ago, and is currently on its way to L2. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/nasa-roman-space-telescope-launch-success-on-a-spacex-falcon-heavy-rocket-spectacular-launch-video"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Space.com}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nasaspaceflight.com/2026/09/roman-commissioning-update/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 NASASpaceFlight}}\

\f2\i Rubin Observatory's LSST
\f0\i0  is the ground-based counterpart. It will image the southern sky repeatedly over ten years and is now in its early operations.\
Together these should measure billions of galaxy shapes. That would bring statistical errors well below the systematics that Stops 3, 4, and 8 identified, so photometric redshifts, blending of overlapping galaxies, intrinsic alignments, and baryons will decide how much they can deliver.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The arc of the story
\f0\b0 \
Kristian and Sachs predicted in 1966 that the lumpy universe would coherently distort distant galaxies. Sixty years later, surveys measure that distortion in over a hundred million galaxies with sub-percent shear calibration, and they test \uc0\u923 CDM at a level comparable to Planck. Each step followed from the one before: the faint blue galaxies gave the sources, Kaiser\'96Squires gave the method, the 2000 detections gave the proof, and a decade of systematics work made precision possible.\
I could put together a one-page timeline of all ten stops as a summary. Would that be useful, or would you rather dig into one of the S\uc0\u8328  explanations, such as baryonic feedback?}